---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
---
<Base
  title="Never load the whole folder — LightPhotos Blog"
  desc="A folder of 8,889 photos used 1.9 GB of memory and crashed the app. The fix is the idea I reuse most in the code. I made the same mistake four times."
  active="blogs"
>
  <main class="lp-page">
    <article class="lp-article">
      <header class="lp-article-head">
        <p class="lp-article-meta"><a href="/blogs.html">Blogs</a> &middot; <time datetime="2026-09-01">September 1, 2026</time></p>
        <h1>Never load the whole folder</h1>
        <p class="lp-article-dek">
          A folder of 8,889 photos used 1.9 GB of memory, and then the app
          crashed. The fix is the idea I reuse most in the code. I made the
          same mistake four times, each time in a different part of the app.
        </p>
        <p class="lp-article-byline">Andrew Yao &middot; 5 min read</p>
      </header>

      <aside class="lp-tldr">
        <p class="lp-tldr-label">TL;DR</p>
        <p>
          LightPhotos crashed on a folder of 8,889 photos. The cause was simple:
          I loaded a thumbnail for every photo. I fixed it. Now the app keeps
          only what is on screen, plus a few extra thumbnails. Then I found the
          same mistake three more times in other parts of the app. If you build
          software for photo libraries, find the places where your code assumes
          that the folder is small.
        </p>
      </aside>

      <figure class="lp-figure">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 640 264" role="img" aria-label="Chart: memory use against the number of photos in a folder. When the app loads all photos, memory increases to 1.9 GB at 8,889 photos, and then the app crashes. When the app loads only what is on screen, memory stays at 110 to 150 MB. Next to the chart is a list of the four parts of the app where I made the same mistake: the grid, the thumbnail cache, the Auto Tone batch, and the large view.">
          <text class="lp-fig-label" x="0" y="12">MEMORY AS THE FOLDER GROWS</text>
          <line class="lp-fig-line" x1="40" y1="26" x2="40" y2="220"></line>
          <line class="lp-fig-line" x1="40" y1="220" x2="370" y2="220"></line>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="48" y="34">memory</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="40" y="238" text-anchor="middle">0</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="330" y="238" text-anchor="middle">8,889</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="370" y="256" text-anchor="end">photos in the folder</text>

          <line class="lp-fig-bad-stroke" x1="40" y1="219" x2="330" y2="60" stroke-width="2"></line>
          <circle class="lp-fig-bad" cx="330" cy="60" r="5"></circle>
          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="316" y="38" text-anchor="end">Load all photos</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s lp-fig-bad" x="316" y="54" text-anchor="end">1.9 GB, then crash</text>

          <rect class="lp-fig-bar" x="40" y="208" width="330" height="3"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="370" y="184" text-anchor="end">Load only what is on screen</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="370" y="200" text-anchor="end">110 to 150 MB at any size</text>

          <text class="lp-fig-label" x="400" y="12">THE SAME MISTAKE, FOUR TIMES</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="400" y="26" width="240" height="48" rx="6"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="414" y="46">1. Grid textures</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="414" y="64">visible rows plus a few more</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="400" y="84" width="240" height="48" rx="6"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="414" y="104">2. Thumbnail cache</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="414" y="122">limit 256 for 180 cells</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="400" y="142" width="240" height="48" rx="6"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="414" y="162">3. Auto Tone batch</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="414" y="180">a window of 32 photos</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="400" y="200" width="240" height="48" rx="6"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="414" y="220">4. Large view</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="414" y="238">size rounds up to 512 px</text>
        </svg>
        <figcaption>
          If the app loads all photos, memory grows with the folder until the
          app crashes. If it keeps only what is on screen, memory stays flat. I
          made the first mistake in four parts of the app.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <h2>What I learned</h2>
      <ul class="lp-article-list">
        <li>
          <strong>Look for loops over everything the user gave you.</strong>
          A loop that handles one request is usually fine. A loop that grows
          with the number of photos a person owns can cause failures.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Size memory by what you must keep at the same time.</strong>
          I asked how many photos were in a job. The better question was how
          long each thumbnail must stay in memory.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Write the calculation next to the number.</strong> A person
          who later reads only the number 32 must know what can make it wrong.
          That person can be me.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Check old numbers again when things change.</strong> Three
          of these four bugs were correct when I wrote them. Later, something
          changed, and I did not look at the code again.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        Imagine that a wedding photographer gives you a card with 8,889 photos
        on it. You open the folder in LightPhotos, and the app crashes.
      </p>
      <p>
        That was a real bug. I did not expect to fix the same bug three more
        times, in places that looked very different from each other.
      </p>

      <h2>The crash</h2>
      <p>
        The grid shows a small preview (a thumbnail) for every photo in the
        folder. My code to fill the grid did approximately this:
      </p>
      <pre class="lp-code">for each photo in folder:
    request a thumbnail
    upload it to the GPU as a texture</pre>
      <p>
        That loop is fine if you have twenty photos. With 8,889 photos, it
        tells the graphics card to hold 8,889 images (textures). These use
        about 1.3 GB of video memory, and the total memory use of the app
        increases to about 1.9 GB. Then the operating system stops the app.
      </p>
      <p>
        The loop does what it says. But it assumes that a folder has only a
        few photos in it, and I did not see that assumption.
      </p>

      <h2>The fix, the first time</h2>
      <p>
        On a large monitor, you can see about forty thumbnails at the same
        time. The other 8,849 are off screen, and nobody needs them yet.
      </p>

