      <p>
        Now the grid draws only the rows that you scrolled to. It asks for
        thumbnails for those rows, plus a few extra rows above and below, so
        that scrolling stays smooth. The app does not load or keep anything
        else. Web developers who built long scrolling tables know this method.
        Its usual name is list virtualisation. I did not stop there, because
        the same problem came back.
      </p>

      <h2>The fix, the second time</h2>
      <p>
        After the app loads a thumbnail, I keep it in a memory cache. The cache
        holds a fixed maximum number of thumbnails. I picked that number a long
        time ago, when thumbnails were small. Later, I made thumbnails bigger:
        a fixed 512 pixels on the long side and about 700 KB each. That is
        roughly seven times the old cost of each thumbnail.
      </p>
      <p>
        Nothing crashed, and no error appeared. The limit was still a valid
        number. But its meaning was very different from what I intended when I
        wrote it.
      </p>
      <p>
        So I calculated the limit again from the value that it must depend on:
        the number of thumbnails that must be in memory at the same time. That
        is the visible grid plus three extra rows above and below, about 180
        cells. So the limit is now 256. If I make it lower, the cache discards
        thumbnails right after it loads them. That is worse than no cache.
      </p>

      <h2>The fix, the third time</h2>
      <p>
        If you select a few thousand photos and click Auto Tone, LightPhotos
        analyses each one to calculate its adjustments. My first version asked
        for a thumbnail for every selected photo at the start, because it would
        need all of them at some point. With 20,000 photos selected, at roughly
        0.4 MB each, I asked the cache to hold about 8 GB. The browser tab has
        a limit of 4 GB, and it never gives memory back.
      </p>
      <p>
        I asked how many photos were in the job. The important question was how
        long each thumbnail must stay in memory.
      </p>

      <figure class="lp-figure">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 640 152" role="img" aria-label="Diagram: a window of 32 photos moves across a selection of 20,000 photos. The app never requests more than 32 thumbnails at the same time.">
          <text class="lp-fig-label" x="0" y="12">AUTO TONE ACROSS 20,000 SELECTED PHOTOS</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="0" y="34" width="640" height="42" rx="6"></rect>
          <rect class="lp-fig-bar-dim" x="1" y="35" width="199" height="40"></rect>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box-accent" x="200" y="26" width="76" height="58" rx="6"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="238" y="60" text-anchor="middle">32</text>
          <line class="lp-fig-line" x1="288" y1="55" x2="332" y2="55"></line>
          <path class="lp-fig-arrow" d="M332 49 L332 61 L344 55 Z"></path>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="100" y="102" text-anchor="middle">already analysed</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="238" y="102" text-anchor="middle">in memory</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="460" y="102" text-anchor="middle">not requested yet</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="0" y="140">Slowest cold RAW decode 137 ms &divide; one analysis 8 ms = 17, rounded up to 32.</text>
        </svg>
        <figcaption>
          About 13 MB of memory, for 30 selected photos or for 30,000. A check
          in the code stops the build if the window is 64 or above. Thus nobody
          can silently change the window into a setting for casual adjustment.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p>
        I did not pick 32 by guess. The analysis of one photo takes about 8 ms.
        It must run on the main thread of the app, so I cannot split it across
        CPU cores. Loading a thumbnail from an uncached RAW file takes 137 ms
        in the slow cases (95 out of 100 loads are faster). The
        loading must stay ahead of the analysis, also in that worst case. Thus
        the window is the slowest load divided by one analysis.
      </p>
      <p>
        Before I measured, I assumed that the thumbnail loads made the batch
        slow. I was wrong. The analysis takes about fifty-five times longer in
        real time, because it cannot run in parallel. Work to speed up the
        loading would not help at all.
      </p>

      <h2>The fix, the fourth time</h2>
      <p>
        When you open a photo large, I load it at a size that fits the window.
        In principle, a one-pixel change to the window size means a new load.
        Now the requested size rounds up to a multiple of 512 pixels. Thus a
        drag of the window edge causes only a few reloads, not one for every
        frame.
      </p>
      <p>
        This is the same idea as the other three fixes. But here it applies to
        a value that changes smoothly (the window size), not to a list. If an
        expensive cache depends on a value like that, I round the value.
      </p>

